// rustc lifetimes/borrowck-let-suggestion.rs — upstream E0716
// imported batch B167 @ da5114692c9ebe46b869488c5f34f92eb10b98c1 (rust 1.100.0)
// shelf: admit · root lifereg.D
// RE-PORTED AS-IS 2026-09-07 (stage 3). The old port was
// `let h: Holder = Holder { p: &mk() };` — a braced-struct OPERAND in a `let`
// initializer, an extending expression in Rust, so it was legal and sat in
// bc_admits_blocked as LEGAL-AS-PORTED. Upstream calls a METHOD ON A TEMPORARY
// RECEIVER and binds the borrow-carrying result; extension does not reach there.
// Original path: tests/ui/lifetimes/borrowck-let-suggestion.rs
// upstream commit: 4b0c9d76ae7d387229caea55cfa73c280b08b8a7
// upstream construct: lines 2-4 —
//     let mut x = vec![1].iter();
//     //~^ ERROR temporary value dropped while borrowed
//     x.use_mut();
// Modifications:
//   · `vec![1]` -> `mkv() -> Vecish`, `.iter()` -> an inherent `iter(&'s self) ->
//     Iter<'s>`; upstream's `Vec`/`slice::Iter` pair is exactly this shape (a
//     receiver temporary and an iterator STRUCT carrying a borrow of it), and the
//     borrow-carrying return type is the whole subject.
//   · the `Fake` blanket-impl trait (`use_mut`) exists upstream only to give the
//     iterator a later use -> `*x.p` reads through the dangling borrow instead
//   · assert -> exit code: `fn main() -> i32`
// ⚠ MEASURED 2026-09-07: the SAME method-on-a-temporary shape IS refused when the
// method returns a bare `&T` ("temporary value dropped while borrowed"); it is
// admitted only when the borrow is carried inside a returned STRUCT. That
// difference is the root, and it is what the old port could not show.
package borrowck_let_suggestion;
struct Vecish { n: i64 }
struct Iter<'a> { p: &'a i64 }
impl Vecish { fn iter<'s>(self: &'s Vecish) -> Iter<'s> { return Iter { p: &self.n }; } }
fn mkv() -> Vecish { return Vecish { n: 1i64 }; }
fn main() -> i32 {
    let mut x: Iter = mkv().iter();   // THE DEFECT: admitted, upstream E0716
    return *x.p as i32;
}
